  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Digital Media Practices in Households shows an early but clear scholarly footprint, with 9 OpenAlex citations and 96 recorded downloads. The book is also openly available, which supports access and reuse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Digital Media Practices in Households is an open-access scholarly book by Larissa Hjorth, Kana Ohashi, Jolynna Sinanan, Heather A. Horst, and Sarah Pink, published in 2025. Its ethnographic focus on household digital life, intergenerational relationships, self-tracking, quantified self practices, and care at a distance positions it at the intersection of media studies, sociology, digital media research, and related social science fields.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The usage record shows 96 downloads, indicating that readers are finding and accessing the book soon after publication. The open-access distribution through multiple repositories strengthens reach by lowering barriers to consultation and reuse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence is still modest but meaningful for a recent monograph: OpenAlex records 9 citations, which points to emerging scholarly uptake rather than mature consolidation. The citing works suggest the book is already being drawn into conversations on digital media, kinship, older adults, legal anthropology, and platformed or invasive online environments, indicating that its household-based analysis is resonating beyond a single subfield.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader record is still relatively thin, so the impact story should be read as early and developing. Even so, the combination of downloads, open availability, and cross-disciplinary citations suggests a book that is already circulating within scholarly communities and beginning to inform adjacent debates on technology, care, and everyday digital practice.</p>",
